{
  "dom_id": "step-q18233",
  "name": "Question 13",
  "problem": "An eigenvector of $D=[\\begin{aligned}-8 & 10 \\\\ -7 & 11\\end{aligned}]$ corresponding to an eigenvalue $\\lambda =6$ is  $[\\begin{aligned}c \\\\ 14\\end{aligned}].$ Determine the value of $c.$",
  "worked_solution": "Remember that the eigenvectors of $D$ are the non-zero vectors $v$ such that $Dv=\\lambda v.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Dv & =\\lambda v \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}-8 & 10 \\\\ -7 & 11\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}c \\\\ 14\\end{aligned}] & =6[\\begin{aligned}c \\\\ 14\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}-8c+140 \\\\ -7c+154\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}6c \\\\ 84\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives us the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-8c+140=6c \\\\ -7c+154=84\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Solving for $c$ in the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n-8c+140=6c\\,\u27f9\\,c=10.\n$$\n\n- Solving for $c$ in the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n-7c+154=84\\,\u27f9\\,c=10.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution of the system is $c=10.$",
